//   ResistanceOn (ohm) = 165000  (borrowed from sot_hanjiang_aipadv2024_16nm (Table I, 'Resistance On (ohm)' = 165000); the source paper does not report it)
// CHECK: assumed (not measured) values: MemCellLevel, ResistanceOff (ohm),
// ResistanceOn (ohm)
// NOTE: NODE TRAP: the paper's F is 30 nm (half metal pitch) while the
// CMOS technology is called 15 nm. Writing -ProcessNode: 15 with
// -CellArea: 20 would make the cell 4x too small in absolute area. Use
// ProcessNode 30. Confirmed independently against Fig. 9(c) absolute areas
// (SOT bar = 0.018 um^2 = 20 x 30^2 nm^2).
// NOTE: No TMR, R_P or R_AP number appears anywhere in the paper. The MTJ
// resistance is taken from a measured RA-vs-oxide-thickness curve in Yuasa
// et al., Nat. Mater. 3:868 (2004) [ref 22] and swept; the chosen SOT
// oxide thickness is never printed either (1.3 nm is stated for STT, 1.6
// nm for VCEC, 1.4 nm for ME). ResistanceOn/ResistanceOff cannot be filled
// from this paper.
// NOTE: Table 4 write/read energies and times are TOTAL ARRAY ACCESS
// values for a 256x128 array, not cell-intrinsic values. They are
// validation targets, not .cell inputs. In particular t_write = 1.39 ns =
// t_WL + t_BL + t_mag; the magnet switching time t_mag alone is never
// printed for the chosen design.
// NOTE: The abstract claims SOT-MRAM suffers 'a 25% larger cell area
// compared with STT-MRAM' - this compares 20 F^2 (SOT, 2T1MTJ) against 16
// F^2 (STT, 2T1MTJ). Against STT 1T1MTJ (12 F^2) the SOT penalty is 67%,
// not 25%.
// NOTE: Bi0.9Sb0.1 row of Table 3 has theta_SH = 52 and I_read/I_write =
// 4.49; the paper itself rejects it on read-disturb grounds. Do not build
// a cell from that row.
